"A soldier and writer who also knew Socrates and wrote his own Apology and Memorabilia. His Socrates is more practical and less philosophical than Platos.": "707336c02832",
"A stonemasons son and soldier who questioned his fellow citizens about justice, courage, and virtue, wrote nothing, and was executed for impiety at about seventy.": "7c648f6ef1f1",
"An amnesty forbade prosecutions for crimes under the Thirty.": "75ae32e7dc3c",
"Cicero later said that Socrates called philosophy down from the heavens. After him, ethics, the question of the good life, stood at the center of philosophy beside the study of nature.": "0a8370ef9b80",
"He drank the hemlock calmly while his friends wept.": "0646a3e5e04c",
"He knows nothing, and thinks that he knows; I neither know nor think that I know.": "70403522254e",
"He refused to compromise, even to save his life.": "6f4d8821c85f",
"Platos Phaedo describes the last day. Socrates spends it discussing whether the soul is immortal, then drinks the hemlock calmly while his friends weep. His last words, in Jowetts translation, were Crito, I owe a cock to Asclepius; will you remember to pay the debt? The narrator ends: of all the men of his time whom I have known, he was the wisest and justest and best. We should remember that this is a portrait by a devoted student, written years later and shaped by Platos own philosophy. Xenophons account of the trial is different in tone, and modern historians debate how much of Platos Socrates is the real man. What no one doubts is the outline: a philosopher was tried, refused to compromise, and died.": "69f30176ee3a",
"Socrates called himself the gadfly of Athens.": "87cc9bb0082a",
"Socrates did not plead for mercy. He told the jury that he was like the gadfly who stirs the generous steed into motion, and that he could not stop philosophizing, because the unexamined life is not worth living. The jury voted to convict, but narrowly. Had thirty votes gone over to the other side, he said, I should have been acquitted. At the penalty stage the prosecution proposed death, and Socrates at first suggested that he deserved free meals at public expense, as an Olympic victor did. More of the jury voted for death than had voted for guilt. His friends arranged an escape, and in Platos Crito he refuses it, arguing that he will not break the laws of the city that raised him.": "2f2354bc0df6",
"Socrates left no system. He left a practice: ask what a word means, test the answer against cases, and admit what you do not know. Teachers still call it the Socratic method.": "77c7eb5878f3",
"Socrates most famous student, who turned from politics after the trial and made his teacher the hero of the dialogues. Our main source, and an interested one.": "5859ac62abac",
"Socrates was a stonemasons son who fought as an infantryman for Athens and spent the rest of his time in conversation. He wrote nothing. We know him through his students, above all Plato and Xenophon, and through a comedy that mocked him, Aristophanes Clouds. In Platos Apology, his speech at the trial, he explains how his mission began. His friend Chaerephon went to the oracle at Delphi and asked whether anyone was wiser than I was, and the Pythian prophetess answered, that there was no man wiser, in Benjamin Jowetts translation. Socrates was puzzled, since he knew he was not wise, and set out to find someone wiser by questioning politicians, poets, and craftsmen.": "f4defa061c2b",
"Socrates was charged with impiety and corrupting the young.": "d6f0df301e0b",
"That outline changed philosophy. Before Socrates, Greek thinkers mostly asked what the world is made of. Socrates turned the question toward how one should live, and he insisted that the answer must survive questioning. His death gave that insistence a terrible seriousness. Plato, who had planned a political career, turned away from politics and spent his life writing dialogues in which Socrates is the hero, and founded a school to continue the conversation. The trial also left a lasting question for democracies. Socrates was condemned by ordinary citizens, by majority vote, in a lawful trial. Whether a free society can tolerate the people who question its deepest beliefs is a question that has been asked again in every century since.": "f72844f88c94",
"The Thirty were a brutal oligarchy installed by Sparta.": "bb43996f6034",
"The formal charge, as Socrates reports it, was that Socrates is a doer of evil, who corrupts the youth; and who does not believe in the gods of the state, but has other new divinities of his own. To understand why a democracy brought it, look at the years before. Athens had just lost a twenty-seven-year war with Sparta. In 404 BCE the Spartans installed an oligarchy, the Thirty, who executed hundreds of citizens before democracy was restored the next year. The leader of the Thirty, Critias, and another of its members had been companions of Socrates. So had Alcibiades, a brilliant general who had betrayed Athens to Sparta. An amnesty forbade prosecutions for political crimes of those years, so the charge was impiety. But to many jurors, the old man who taught young aristocrats to question everything must have looked dangerous.": "73837f21eddd",
"The great comic playwright whose Clouds (423 BCE) showed Socrates hanging in a basket studying the sky and teaching students to win unjust arguments. Socrates says the play shaped the prejudice against him.": "1267326aa062",
"The hour of departure has arrived, and we go our ways—I to die, and you to live. Which is better God only knows.": "56cc06ed0917",
"The indictment accused him of introducing new divinities.": "624292e4e013",
"The trial made the conflict between free inquiry and public opinion a permanent theme. Platos Republic, Mills On Liberty, and modern debates about academic freedom all return to it.": "430799c2898b",
"The unexamined life is not worth living.": "62e857c0bd13",
"Thirty more votes would have acquitted him.": "1aff151aa714",
"What he found made him enemies. Each expert claimed to know things he did not know, and when Socrates questioned him in public, the claim fell apart. His conclusion was modest and devastating: he knows nothing, and thinks that he knows; I neither know nor think that I know. This was the meaning of the oracle. His only wisdom was knowing the limits of his knowledge. Young men from wealthy families followed him around and imitated his method on their elders, which did not make him more popular. To many Athenians he looked like the character in Aristophanes play, a clever talker who studied the sky and could make the worse appear the better cause.": "68704955bf07",
